```apex
/**
 * Emails Opportunity owners that a renewal is due.
 *
 * Do not call Messaging.sendEmail once per Opportunity. A transaction may invoke
 * sendEmail at most 10 times, and each invocation accepts at most 100 messages.
 * The scheduled job passes up to 200 Ids, so a per-record call throws an uncatchable
 * LimitException on the 11th invocation. Messaging dispatches mail only when the
 * transaction commits, so that exception rolls back every send already handed over
 * and nothing goes out. That is the opposite of isolating a single failure.
 *
 * Partial success is sendEmail(messages, false) (allOrNone = false), in batches of
 * 100. Each SendEmailResult reports that message; one bad recipient does not cancel
 * the rest, and two invocations cover the job's 200-Id maximum.
 *
 * setTargetObjectId(owner Id) with setSaveAsActivity(false) is an internal-user send
 * and does not consume the org's daily allocation for external email. WhatId is not
 * set: it is not allowed when the target is a User. Queue-owned opportunities are
 * skipped because setTargetObjectId does not accept a Group Id.
 */
public inherited sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    // Platform maximum for one Messaging.sendEmail invocation.
    private static final Integer MAX_EMAILS_PER_SEND = 100;

    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        Set<Id> ownerIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Opportunity opportunity : opportunities) {
            if (isUser(opportunity.OwnerId)) {
                ownerIds.add(opportunity.OwnerId);
            }
        }
        if (ownerIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        Map<Id, User> ownersById = new Map<Id, User>([
            SELECT Id, IsActive, Email
            FROM User
            WHERE Id IN :ownerIds
        ]);

        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages = new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        for (Opportunity opportunity : opportunities) {
            User owner = ownersById.get(opportunity.OwnerId);
            if (owner == null || !owner.IsActive || String.isBlank(owner.Email)) {
                continue;
            }
            messages.add(buildMessage(opportunity));
        }

        return sendAll(messages);
    }

    private static Boolean isUser(Id ownerId) {
        return ownerId != null && ownerId.getSObjectType() == User.SObjectType;
    }

    private static Messaging.SingleEmailMessage buildMessage(Opportunity opportunity) {
        Messaging.SingleEmailMessage message = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
        message.setTargetObjectId(opportunity.OwnerId);
        message.setSaveAsActivity(false);
        message.setUseSignature(false);
        message.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opportunity.Name);
        message.setPlainTextBody(buildBody(opportunity));
        return message;
    }

    private static String buildBody(Opportunity opportunity) {
        String closeDateText = opportunity.CloseDate == null
            ? 'not set'
            : String.valueOf(opportunity.CloseDate);
        return 'The renewal for "' + opportunity.Name
            + '" is due. Close date: ' + closeDateText + '.';
    }

    private static Integer sendAll(List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages) {
        Integer sentCount = 0;
        for (Integer startIndex = 0; startIndex < messages.size(); startIndex += MAX_EMAILS_PER_SEND) {
            Integer endIndex = Math.min(startIndex + MAX_EMAILS_PER_SEND, messages.size());
            List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> batch = new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
            for (Integer index = startIndex; index < endIndex; index++) {
                batch.add(messages[index]);
            }
            // false = allOrNone. A new list per batch: do not clear a list already passed to sendEmail.
            sentCount += countSuccessful(Messaging.sendEmail(batch, false));
        }
        return sentCount;
    }

    private static Integer countSuccessful(List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> results) {
        Integer sentCount = 0;
        if (results == null) {
            return sentCount;
        }
        for (Messaging.SendEmailResult result : results) {
            if (result != null && result.isSuccess()) {
                sentCount++;
            }
        }
        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

The unfiltered `Contact` query is not bulk-safe here, so the handler does not do that. An Apex transaction can read at most 50,000 rows; this org has about 2.3 million contacts, so `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]` throws `System.LimitException` on every run, and it would also blow the 6 MB heap limit. Bulk-safe means one query scoped to the records in this trigger chunk (MDM batches of 200), not one query of the whole table. Contacts are loaded only for accounts whose `BillingCountry` actually changed, then filtered in memory, then updated in one DML. The class is `without sharing` so the MDM user updates every related contact, not only rows that user can see.

Same-transaction work still cannot update more than 10,000 contacts at once. A headquarters move on an account larger than that needs Batch Apex; that is outside this trigger.

